.TH 9SHARE 8 "2026" "9share" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
9share \- share directories between machines over 9P, through ssh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B 9share setup
.br
.B 9share export
.I DIR HOST
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B sudo 9share mount
.I USER NAME DIR
.br
.B 9share pair
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.I NAME HOST
.br
.B 9share unexport
.I NAME
.br
.B sudo 9share unmount
.I NAME
.br
.B 9share
.BR rw | ro | off
.I NAME
.br
.B 9share status
.br
.B sudo 9share wsl\-boot
.RB [ \-\-remove ]
.br
.B 9share boot
.RB [ \-\-detach ]
.br
.B 9share serve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B 9share
makes a directory on one machine appear at a mount point on another, through ssh: on the machine that has the files you name the
directory and the other machine, and on the other you name where it should appear.  The machine with the files dials out, so the
other needs no open port and no server to configure, and a connection that drops is picked up again where it stopped.  Install the
same package on both machines; nothing happens on a machine until something is configured on it.  The file server is
.BR daud (8),
a fork of diod, run as the exporting user.
.SH QUICK START
Install the package on both machines, and make
.B ssh HOST true
work without a password from the machine that has the files to the one that should show them.  Then, two commands.
.PP
On the machine that has the files, as yourself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
9share export /srv/projects bob.example.org
.fi
.RE
.PP
On
.B bob.example.org
(the command is printed by the first one, ready to copy), once, as root:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo 9share mount alice projects /mnt/projects
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I /srv/projects
now appears at
.IR /mnt/projects ,
and stays there: the machine with the files dials out, so
.B bob.example.org
needs no open port, and a connection that drops is picked up again where it stopped.
.I alice
is the account on
.B bob.example.org
that ssh logs in as (the one you would name in
.BR "ssh alice@bob.example.org" ),
and
.I projects
is the name of the share, by default the directory's own name.
.PP
Neither machine has to be configured first, and nothing happens on a machine until something is.  To be asked instead of typing
these, run
.B 9share setup
on either machine: it asks three questions and does the same.  To make each connection prove a secret as well as trusting ssh,
run
.B 9share pair projects bob.example.org
once.  Switch the mount, without sudo, with
.BR "9share ro projects" ,
.B "9share rw projects"
or
.BR "9share off projects" ;
.B 9share status
shows what is shared, what is mounted and what is running.
.SH HOW IT WORKS
The 9P connection of daud rides a
.IR "resumable link" :
each end counts the bytes it has sent and keeps those the other has not acknowledged, so when ssh breaks and is run again, the
two ends say how much each has received and resend the rest.  Nothing is lost, nothing is delivered twice, and the connection and
the files it has open do not notice.  The mount stays while the connection is down, and what the kernel asks waits, for up to the
.B hold
time of the share (600 seconds unless the configuration says otherwise).  After that the session is given up, the mount is
removed, and a new one begins when the exporter is back.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Setting up
.TP
.B setup
Ask what is needed and write the simple, usual configuration: on the machine that has the files, which directory, shared with
which machine, under which name, and whether to make a secret as well; on the machine that shows them, which account, which share,
and where it appears.  Or give the answers as arguments:
.B "9share setup \-\-export DIR HOST [NAME] [\-\-no\-pair]"
or
.BR "9share setup \-\-mount USER NAME DIR" .
See
.BR 9share\-setup (1).
.SS On the machine that has the files (as an ordinary user)
.TP
.BI "export " "DIR HOST " [ NAME ]
Share DIR with HOST (a name as ssh knows it) as NAME, by default the directory's own name.  The block is added to
.IR ~/.config/9share/9share.conf ;
the running service notices within a few seconds.  Prints the command to run on HOST.
.TP
.BI unexport " NAME"
Stop sharing it.
.TP
.BR pair " [\fB\-\-force\fP] \fINAME HOST\fP"
Make a random secret for the share and put it on both machines, in
.I ~/.config/9share/NAME.secret
(mode 0600), the one here and the one on HOST (in the home of the account that ssh logs in as there), through ssh, with the secret
on standard input and never on a command line.  From then on every connection proves the secret to the other end with
SCRAM-SHA-256 as well as trusting ssh; the export here and the mount there pick it up by themselves.  The other machine is done
first, so that a failure leaves this one as it was.  A secret that exists on either machine is not replaced without
.BR \-\-force .
HOST needs 9share installed.  Without a secret file the link trusts ssh alone.  A secret file that exists but cannot be used (not
the owner's, or readable by others) stops the share rather than falling back to trusting ssh.
.SS On the machine that shows them
.TP
.BI "mount " "USER NAME DIR"
(root) Show the share NAME that arrives in the account USER of this machine (the account the exporter logs in as) at DIR.  The block
is added to
.IR /etc/9share/9share.conf .
It is mounted while the exporter's connection is up and unmounted when the session is given up.
.TP
.BI unmount " NAME"
(root) Remove it and unmount.
.TP
.BR rw | ro | off " \fINAME\fP"
(as USER, no sudo) Mounted read-write (the default), read-only, or not mounted.  Follows within a few seconds.
.SS On either
.TP
.B status
What is shared, what is mounted, and whether the daemon runs.
.TP
.BR wsl\-boot " [\fB\-\-remove\fP]"
(root, WSL2) Put the daemon in the [boot] command of
.IR /etc/wsl.conf ,
which WSL2 has instead of systemd.  A command already there is kept and runs first, from
.IR /etc/9share/wsl\-boot .
.B \-\-remove
takes 9share out again and gives the command that was kept back.  The package does this by itself; see below.
.SS Behind these
.TP
.B boot
The root daemon, run by the
.B 9share
service (or with
.BR \-\-detach ,
in the background, logging to /var/log/9share.log).  Every two seconds it starts
.B serve
for each user who has a configuration file and no
.B serve
running, and for each mount of the system configuration it keeps a relay (below) and the kernel's end of a socket pair; when the
relay reports that the exporter's session has begun it mounts over that socket (with the 9p option
.BR trans=fd ),
and when the relay ends it unmounts.  It follows each user's mode.  With nothing configured it has nothing to do.
.TP
.B serve
One user's exports, in the foreground.  For each export it runs
.B "daud \-\-dial"
with
.B ssh
to the other machine as the carrier; ssh runs
.B "9share link NAME"
there.  It is started again when it dies, after 1, 2, 4 ... 30 seconds; ssh runs with ServerAliveInterval 15 and ServerAliveCountMax
3, so a dead connection is noticed in about 45 seconds and a new one is made.  The configuration file is read again whenever it
changes, and only the exports whose block changed are restarted; so is the export whose secret file appears, changes or goes.
.TP
.B relay
The receiving end of one mount, run by the daemon as the mount's user: it holds the link's session and one end of the socket pair, and
listens on
.IR ~/.9share/NAME.link .
It accepts a carrier only from a process of that user, and with a secret file only from one that proves the secret; the secret is
read again for each carrier.
.TP
.B link
What ssh runs on the receiving machine: joins its standard input and output to the relay's socket.  It is a pipe, and nothing else.
.SH STARTING AT BOOT
The package finds out how the machine starts services and does the right thing; it never fails the installation, and says what it
could not do.
.TP
.B systemd
The unit
.B 9share.service
is enabled and started.  (This includes WSL2 when
.I /etc/wsl.conf
says systemd=true.)
.TP
.B WSL2 without systemd
.B 9share wsl\-boot
is run, and the daemon is started at once.
.TP
.B sysvinit, OpenRC
The init script
.I /etc/init.d/9share
is enabled and started (with
.B update\-rc.d
or
.BR rc\-update ).
.TP
.B runit, or no init system at all (a container)
Nothing is set up, and the message says to run
.B "sudo 9share boot \-\-detach"
or to write the service.
.PP
The helper that does this,
.IR 9share\-init ,
is installed in the libexec directory of 9share (for the package,
.IR /usr/lib/9share ),
and can be run again:
.B "9share\-init detect"
prints the kind it found;
.B "9share\-init enable"
and
.B "9share\-init disable"
do and undo it.  See
.BR 9share\-init (8).
.SH SECURITY
Mounts are made nosuid,nodev, with access=any: any user on the receiving machine who can reach the mount point sees the files with
the exporting user's rights.  The receiving machine mounts only over a connection that the relay of the mount's user accepted, which
is a process of that user; with a secret (see
.BR pair )
only one that proved the secret as well.  Every value that goes from the exporter into a mount option is checked first.  Do not
export a directory to a machine whose users you do not trust.
.PP
The secret protects the link against another process that can reach the relay's socket, or an ssh login that is not the exporter's.
It does not make the 9P traffic secret: that is ssh's work.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NINESHARE_SYSCONFDIR
Where the system configuration is looked for (default: the sysconfdir given to configure, plus /9share).  For tests.
.TP
.B NINESHARE_DAUD
The daud program to run (default: the one installed with 9share).  For tests.
.TP
.B NINESHARE_WSL_CONF
The file that
.B wsl\-boot
edits (default /etc/wsl.conf).  For tests.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/9share/9share.conf
.TQ
.I /etc/9share/9share.conf
The exports of a user and the mounts of a machine.  The package installs the second as a commented template with no blocks.  See
.BR 9share.conf (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/9share/NAME.secret
The secret of a share, one line, mode 0600, made by
.BR "9share pair" .
There is one on each machine; the file's presence turns on SCRAM-SHA-256 for that share.
.TP
.I ~/.config/9share/mode.d/NAME
The mode (rw, ro, off) of a mount, written by
.BR "9share rw|ro|off" .
.TP
.I ~/.9share/NAME.link
On the receiving machine: the unix socket of the relay of a mount.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/9share/NAME/daud.log
The log of the daud of an export.
.TP
.I /var/log/9share.log
The log of
.B "9share boot \-\-detach" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR 9share\-setup (1),
.BR 9share\-init (8),
.BR 9share.conf (5),
.BR daud (8),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR mount (8)
